title: Cortonwood
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and in_campaign = 0 and time >= 74 and time <= 86 and miners_strike = 0
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
// how strong the strike will be: the NUM's mood and union power push it up;
// coal stocked at the power stations and a nuclear programme pull it down
var s = 55 + 0.5 * (Q.num_mood - 60) + 0.3 * (Q.union_power - 40) - 10 * Q.coal_stocks - (Q.nuclear_programme ? 6 : 0);
s += Q.miners_win ? 8 : 0;
s -= Q.miners_beaten ? 8 : 0;
Q.strike_strength = Math.max(10, Math.min(90, s));
!}
face-image: img/events/pit_rally.jpg

= Cortonwood

The National Coal Board, now run by Ian MacGregor, has announced that it will close Cortonwood colliery in South Yorkshire, and twenty more pits this year. The Yorkshire miners have walked out, and [+ num_leader +] has called the rest of the coalfields out with them, area by area, without a national ballot.

The power stations hold [? if coal_stocks >= 3 : enough coal to last the winter and beyond ?][? if coal_stocks = 2 : a good deal of coal, though not enough for a long winter ?][? if coal_stocks <= 1 : too little coal for a long dispute ?].

- @stand_firm: Back the Coal Board. The closures go ahead.
- @withdraw_plan: Withdraw the closure plan, as in 1981.

@stand_firm
subtitle: The longest strike in British history begins.
on-arrival: miners_strike = 1; strike_months = 0; strike_wave += 3; num_mood += 10; dry_dissent -= 4; wet_dissent += 3

The miners in Nottinghamshire vote to keep working; most of the rest come out. Flying pickets set off for the working pits before dawn. The government says it is a matter for the Coal Board, and everyone knows it is not.

@withdraw_plan
subtitle: Peace in the coalfields. The dries call it 1981 all over again.
on-arrival: miners_strike = -1; num_mood -= 10; union_power += 5; dry_dissent += 10; thatcher_rating -= 2; budget -= 1

The closures are withdrawn and the Coal Board gets more money. The NUM claims a second victory over the government in three years, and starts to believe it can never lose.

#################### During the strike

@orgreave
title: Orgreave
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and miners_strike = 1 and strike_months >= 2 and orgreave_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: orgreave_seen = 1
face-image: img/events/pit_rally.jpg

= Orgreave

Thousands of pickets have converged on the coking plant at Orgreave, near Sheffield, to stop the lorries carrying coke to the Scunthorpe steelworks. Thousands of police in riot gear, some of them on horseback, are there to keep the gates open. The pictures on the evening news are of mounted charges and men with blood on their faces.

- @back_police: Back the police.
- @urge_restraint: Call for calm on both sides.

@back_police
subtitle: The right approves. The pickets don't get through. The wets wince.
on-arrival: strike_strength -= 6; union_backlash += 4; num_mood += 5; right_dissent -= 3; wet_dissent += 2; lib_relation -= 2

You tell the Commons that what we have seen is an attempt to substitute the rule of the mob for the rule of law, and that it must not succeed. The lorries keep moving.

@urge_restraint
subtitle: The wets approve. The pickets take heart.
on-arrival: strike_strength += 4; right_dissent += 4; wet_dissent -= 2

The Home Office calls for restraint from pickets and police alike. The NUM reads it as a sign that the government's nerve is going.

@working_miners
title: The Working Miners
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and miners_strike = 1 and strike_months >= 3 and working_miners_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: working_miners_seen = 1
face-image: img/events/pit_rally.jpg

= The Working Miners

Most of Nottinghamshire's miners are still at work. They were never given a national ballot, and they say they won't strike without one. Some of them have gone to the High Court to have the strike declared unofficial in their area.

- @back_working: Back the working miners and their court cases.
- @leave_coal_board: Leave it to the Coal Board.

@back_working
subtitle: The strike weakens. The NUM is furious.
on-arrival: strike_strength -= 8; num_mood += 5; union_power -= 2; moderates_mood -= 3; wet_dissent += 1

The courts rule that the strike in Nottinghamshire and Derbyshire is unofficial. The working miners begin to talk of a union of their own.

@leave_coal_board
on-arrival: strike_strength -= 2

The government stays out of it, officially. The working miners carry on working, unofficially encouraged.

@sequestration
title: The NUM's Funds
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and miners_strike = 1 and strike_months >= 6 and employment_acts >= 2 and sequestration_seen = 0
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: sequestration_seen = 1
face-image: img/events/westminster.jpg

= The NUM's Funds

The High Court has fined the NUM for contempt after it ignored a ruling that the strike breaches its own rules. The union won't pay. Under the Employment Acts, its funds can now be seized, and the NUM has already moved some of them abroad.

- @seize_funds: Let the courts seize the NUM's funds.
- @hold_back: Hold back, for fear of making martyrs.

@seize_funds
subtitle: The strike is starved of money. The TUC protests; the wets wince.
on-arrival: strike_strength -= 10; union_power -= 4; tuc_mood += 5; wet_dissent += 3; right_dissent -= 2
achievement: sequestration

Sequestrators take control of the NUM's assets and start tracing the money sent abroad. The union can no longer pay its pickets' petrol or its strikers' rent.

@hold_back
subtitle: The strike has more time.
on-arrival: strike_strength += 3; right_dissent += 2

The Attorney General lets it be known that the government has no wish to see the courts used as a weapon. The NUM's money stays where it is.

@nacods
title: The Pit Deputies
